"""Authored reasoning, language, poetry, and communication task records."""

def register(domain, add):
    domain('GRE','Reasoning and Decision Clarity','Reasoning & Critical Thinking','Clarify arguments, alternatives, uncertainty, and decision implications using explicit evidence and counterexamples.',
           'Separate an argument structure from the truth of its premises. A valid-looking diagram does not verify an observation. Preserve the speaker exact claim before making it stronger or weaker. Define terms when their meaning changes the inference. Use counterexamples to test a stated generalization, not to dismiss a narrower claim it never made. Describe uncertainty in words when no defensible numerical probability model is supplied. These tasks support reasoning about ordinary decisions and do not substitute for professional judgment in consequential domains.')
    add('GRE','argument-premise-map | Argument premise map | Argument text;Stated conclusion;Source context | Identify the conclusion the speaker actually asserts~Separate supporting premises from background or rhetorical emphasis~Show which premises support each intermediate claim~Label necessary unstated premises as reconstructions rather than quotations~Check whether the conclusion still follows when a disputed premise is withheld | a premise is unstated=>label it reconstructed and ask whether the speaker accepts it=>an analyst should not attribute invented wording~the argument contains several conclusions=>map each separately=>one supporting chain may not establish all of them | Claim;Role;Support relationship;Open question | Fictional reviewer Mina hears that a room is unused on Fridays so a workshop should move there, but room suitability is not discussed. | Room unused Friday;Stated premise;Supports availability only;Is the observation current?~Room suitable for workshop;Reconstructed premise;Needed for recommendation;Does the speaker accept this condition? | confusing a proposed hidden premise with something the speaker said | Fictional analyst Eli receives a recommendation supported by an observation that addresses only one of its necessary conditions.')
    add('GRE','claim-scope-qualification | Claim scope qualification | Original claim;Supporting observations;Intended audience | Extract the population, conditions and time scope implied by the wording~Compare each scope dimension with the supporting observations~Identify which parts of the statement extend beyond the evidence~Draft a narrower claim retaining the useful supported content~List evidence needed to justify any broader version | evidence comes from one setting=>limit the claim to that setting=>an example does not establish universality~a qualifier changes the intended decision=>explain the consequence=>precision must remain understandable to the audience | Claim element;Evidence scope;Revision;Remaining test | Fictional editor Rina reads all users prefer the new form although feedback came only from returning users in a pilot. | Population;Returning pilot users;Say returning pilot participants;New users unknown~Preference;Feedback supplied;Report their expressed preference;Do not infer completion behavior | adding vague words such as may without specifying the actual evidence boundary | Fictional writer Theo wants a universal claim preserved even though the supplied evidence covers a narrow group.')
    add('GRE','alternative-explanation-test | Alternative explanation test | Observation;Preferred explanation;Contextual changes | State the observation separately from the explanation~Generate plausible alternatives tied to supplied context rather than arbitrary speculation~Identify observations that would differ if each explanation were correct~Choose a feasible discriminating check~Retain unresolved alternatives until that check provides evidence | alternatives predict the same observed fact=>seek a different discriminating observation=>repeating the original fact cannot decide between them~an alternative is merely imaginable=>label it speculative=>logical possibility alone does not make it equally supported | Explanation;Predicted difference;Available evidence;Discriminating check | Fictional organizer Tara sees fewer late arrivals after changing reminders and also moving the meeting time. | Reminder effect;People saw reminder and adjusted;Not recorded;Ask about reminder use~Time effect;New time removes conflict;Not recorded;Check prior scheduling conflicts | listing many explanations without identifying how to distinguish them | Fictional manager Ivo attributes an improvement to one change while another relevant condition changed simultaneously.')
    add('GRE','definition-disambiguation | Definition disambiguation | Disputed terms;Example statements;Decision affected | Identify the term used with different meanings~Collect examples each speaker includes or excludes~Write operational definitions preserving those differences~Restate the disagreement under each definition~Determine whether a factual disagreement remains after meanings are aligned | speakers use different definitions=>separate meanings before adjudicating truth=>they may not be contradicting the same proposition~a definition excludes inconvenient examples without rationale=>surface that choice=>a boundary decision should not disguise a conclusion | Term use;Included cases;Operational meaning;Effect on dispute | Fictional colleagues Asha and Noel disagree whether a task is complete: Asha means drafted and Noel means approved. | Asha complete;Draft exists;Drafting finished;Approval remains separate~Noel complete;Approval recorded;Ready for use;Requires reviewer evidence | announcing who is right before identifying what each person means | Fictional team members dispute readiness while using the word for different workflow states.')
    add('GRE','counterexample-generalization-check | Counterexample generalization check | General claim;Proposed counterexample;Claim conditions | Rewrite the generalization with its actual quantifier and conditions~Check whether the example satisfies those conditions~Determine whether it contradicts the exact claim or only a stronger version~Revise the claim minimally if the counterexample is genuine~Preserve the distinction between refuting a universal and estimating a tendency | the claim says always and an in-scope exception exists=>reject the universal wording=>one genuine exception contradicts an unconditional universal~the claim describes a tendency=>do not treat one exception as a prevalence estimate=>frequency needs broader evidence | Statement;Counterexample fit;Logical result;Revision | Fictional speaker Mira says every completed checklist proves delivery, but a supplied checklist was completed before the customer could access the files. | Every checklist proves delivery;In-scope completed checklist;Universal contradicted;Require outcome evidence~Observed case;Access unavailable;Activity not delivery proof;Check final customer access | using an out-of-scope example to reject the actual claim | Fictional debater Ren challenges a qualified claim with an example outside its stated conditions.')
    add('GRE','analogy-transfer-review | Analogy transfer review | Proposed analogy;Source mechanism;Target conditions | Identify the relationship that makes the source example work~Map relevant source and target features separately from superficial similarities~List target differences that could break the mechanism~State which conclusion can transfer conditionally~Design a small target-context check before relying on the analogy | only surface features match=>withhold the transferred conclusion=>similar appearance does not establish a shared mechanism~a key enabling condition differs=>make that dependency explicit=>the source result may rely on it | Source feature;Target counterpart;Transfer status;Check | Fictional organizer Lila wants to copy a small club signup process for a larger public event, where identities and access needs differ. | Simple signups;Public attendees;Partial similarity;Check required information~Known participants;Unfamiliar attendees;Enabling condition differs;Test verification and access handling | copying a successful practice without identifying why it worked | Fictional team copies a process from another organization based only on a similar name.')
    add('GRE','decision-reversibility-map | Decision reversibility map | Proposed choice;Commitment consequences;Available exit options | Separate preparation from the binding commitment~List what can be undone and what costs or effects remain~Identify dependencies that make a superficially reversible choice harder to unwind~Choose a staged commitment where it preserves the intended learning~Define evidence and authority required before the next stage | a trial creates obligations beyond the trial=>include those residual commitments=>calling something a pilot does not make it reversible~reversal would harm affected people=>include their perspective in the decision=>technical rollback does not erase social consequences | Decision stage;Reversible part;Residual effect;Gate | Fictional group Cedar Lantern considers a trial room booking that requires a nonrefundable supplied deposit. | Reserve room;Booking can be cancelled;Deposit remains spent;Confirm loss acceptance~Trial label;Activity may stop;Payment obligation persists;Do not call cost-free | treating cancellation as proof that no consequence remains | Fictional planner describes a trial as risk-free although its supplied terms include lasting commitments.')
    add('GRE','uncertainty-to-information-question | Uncertainty to information question | Pending decision;Unknown facts;Available information sources | Distinguish uncertainty that can change the decision from background curiosity~Write each unknown as an answerable question~Specify the possible answers and their decision implications~Choose a source capable of resolving the question~Stop gathering information when additional answers would not change the current choice | an answer would not affect any option=>defer the question=>information collection has an opportunity cost~the proposed source cannot know the fact=>choose another source or retain uncertainty=>confidence does not create access to evidence | Unknown;Possible implication;Source;Priority | Fictional coordinator Elin chooses a meeting room and lacks its verified access arrangements; the wallpaper color is also unknown. | Access arrangement;May rule out room;Venue accessibility contact;Decision-critical~Wallpaper color;No stated decision effect;Venue photo if later useful;Defer | gathering all available facts without connecting them to the choice | Fictional planner researches decorative details while a mandatory access condition remains unresolved.')
    add('GRE','evidence-update-ledger | Evidence update ledger | Prior judgment;New evidence;Competing hypotheses | State the prior judgment and its supporting reasons without inventing a numerical probability~Check whether new evidence is independent or repeats an existing source~Identify which hypotheses would make the evidence more or less expected~Record the direction and scope of the update~State what evidence would reverse or strengthen the revised judgment | several reports repeat one original source=>count the shared origin explicitly=>repetition is not independent corroboration~new evidence addresses only part of a claim=>update only that part=>a local correction should not imply total reversal | Evidence;Independence;Judgment change;Remaining uncertainty | Fictional reviewer Pavi sees several posts claiming a venue is closed, but all cite the same unverified message. | Several posts;Shared original;No independent corroboration;Original message unverified~Official venue reply;Not yet supplied;Could change status judgment;Request current confirmation | treating repeated copies as independent evidence | Fictional analyst doubles confidence after reading duplicated versions of the same report.')
    add('GRE','disagreement-crux-map | Disagreement crux map | Competing positions;Reasons from each side;Shared decision | Restate each position in wording its holder would recognize~Separate factual disputes from value priorities and definition differences~Identify a premise whose resolution could change a participant position~Ask each participant whether the proposed crux is actually decision-relevant~Plan a fair check or explicit tradeoff discussion | disagreement concerns values rather than a fact=>make the tradeoff visible=>more data alone may not choose priorities~neither participant would change position if a premise changed=>seek a different crux=>a debating point is not necessarily decisive | Disagreement;Type;Potential crux;Resolution route | Fictional colleagues Noor and Bea choose between a quiet room and a central room; Noor prioritizes concentration and Bea prioritizes easy attendance. | Room preference;Value priority;Which outcome matters more for this session?;Agree meeting purpose~Travel burden;Factual question;Does central location materially change attendance?;Ask affected attendees | assuming every disagreement can be settled by finding one fact | Fictional team argues about an option while disagreeing about the purpose the option should serve.')

    domain('GLA','Language Transfer and Translation','Languages & Translation','Preserve meaning, register, terminology and uncertainty across languages and learning contexts.', 'Collect source and target language, intended reader and purpose. Distinguish a translation from an explanation or adaptation. Never invent a target-language equivalent when competence or evidence is insufficient: mark the phrase for review by a qualified speaker. Treat examples here as fictional English source revisions or English glosses, not verified translations. Preserve negation, modality, referents and names before improving fluency.')
    add('GLA','translation-brief-preparation | Translation brief preparation | Source material;Target language and audience;Intended use | Identify whether the request needs translation, adaptation or explanation~Record audience knowledge and expected register~List elements that must remain unchanged such as names and identifiers~Identify ambiguous passages that require author clarification~Produce a brief separating mandatory constraints from optional stylistic preferences | target audience is unspecified=>ask before choosing register=>social context affects wording~source meaning is unresolved=>record a translator query=>fluent guessing can conceal ambiguity | Brief element;Supplied constraint;Translation implication;Open question | Fictional editor Mira needs a volunteer notice translated for newcomers, but the target language is not given. | Audience;Newcomers;Explain unfamiliar local terms;Which terms need explanation?~Target language;Missing;Do not translate yet;Which language and regional variety? | selecting a target language from the author name | Fictional coordinator asks for a translated notice but supplies no destination language.')
    add('GLA','translation-ambiguity-query-log | Translation ambiguity query log | Source passage;Available context;Author contact route | Locate phrases with more than one plausible reading~Write the competing meanings in plain language~Identify contextual evidence that favors or excludes each reading~Draft a focused author query without implying one answer~Track resolved and unresolved meanings separately in the translation handoff | ambiguity changes responsibility or timing=>block final wording for that passage=>a wrong reading changes the instruction~context resolves a pronoun clearly=>record the referent=>not every theoretical ambiguity needs an interruption | Source phrase;Possible readings;Evidence;Query | Fictional author Ren writes Tell Alex after Sam approves it; neither it nor the approved object is defined in the supplied excerpt. | It;Message or proposed change;No preceding object supplied;What does Sam approve?~After approval;Timing dependency;Explicit after;Preserve approval before notification | silently choosing the easiest interpretation | Fictional translator receives a pronoun that could refer to either of two prior objects.')
    add('GLA','translation-negation-modality-check | Translation negation and modality check | Source and target drafts;Instruction context;Known terminology | Extract negation, permission, obligation and possibility expressions~Align each expression with its target counterpart~Check the scope of negation over actions and conditions~Compare who is allowed or required to act~Flag altered commitments for bilingual review before stylistic polishing | may becomes must=>flag obligation escalation=>permission and obligation differ~not required becomes prohibited=>restore the distinction=>absence of duty does not imply a ban | Source meaning;Draft meaning;Change;Correction instruction | Fictional bilingual reviewer Lio supplies English glosses: source You may submit a note; target You must submit a note. | May submit;Must submit;Permission became obligation;Restore optional submission~Note;Note;Object preserved;Keep same referent | accepting fluent wording with changed duty | Fictional reviewer sees not required rendered as not allowed in a supplied target-language gloss.')
    add('GLA','translation-terminology-decision-log | Translation terminology decision log | Recurring source terms;Approved references;Target audience | Identify terms whose inconsistency would change understanding~Collect approved equivalents with source and context~Separate fixed identifiers from translatable descriptions~Record disputed or unavailable equivalents as review items~Apply chosen equivalents consistently while preserving context-dependent exceptions | one source term has different meanings=>split entries by context=>uniform spelling need not mean uniform meaning~no approved equivalent is available=>retain a marked query=>invented terminology can mislead downstream readers | Term;Context;Decision;Review evidence | Fictional editor Nia has an approved glossary preserving River Desk as a service name and translating its descriptive label separately. | River Desk;Service name;Preserve exact name;Approved fictional glossary~Service description;Explanatory label;Translate for audience;Target wording requires review | translating an official identifier as ordinary prose | Fictional translation team uses one term for both a user action and an account status.')
    add('GLA','translation-register-alignment | Translation register alignment | Source intention;Audience relationship;Target draft gloss | Identify the relationship between sender and reader~Separate respectfulness from unnecessary formality~Mark wording that changes warmth, distance or authority~Propose consistent register directions with examples of intended effect~Ask a competent target-language reviewer to check social appropriateness | audience relationships differ=>prepare distinct variants=>one register may not fit every recipient~politeness obscures an essential instruction=>make the action explicit=>courtesy should not erase meaning | Feature;Current effect;Target effect;Revision direction | Fictional coordinator Asha writes to familiar volunteers using an unexpectedly severe instruction: You are hereby ordered to reply. | Ordered;Coercive authority;Friendly coordination;Please reply when you can confirm.~Hereby;Ceremonial distance;Plain direct request;Remove ceremonial wording | equating longer wording with greater respect | Fictional editor wants a friendly peer message translated as a formal command.')
    add('GLA','translation-proper-name-number-check | Translation names and quantities check | Source and target drafts;Identifier conventions;Unit requirements | Extract names, identifiers, quantities and dates as paired items~Compare each pair without normalizing away meaningful characters~Separate formatting changes from value changes~Verify requested unit conversions from supplied values and formulas~Return discrepancies with exact source locations and unresolved conventions | a number differs without a documented conversion=>flag it for correction=>fluency cannot justify a changed value~date format is ambiguous=>spell out the month or request context=>different conventions can imply different dates | Item;Source;Draft;Check result | Fictional notice lists room B12 and three folders; its supplied draft gloss lists room B21 and three folders. | Room;B12;B21;Identifier transposed~Folders;Three;Three;Quantity preserved | silently repairing an unfamiliar identifier from memory | Fictional reviewer sees the same quantity but a changed room code in a translation.')
    add('GLA','translation-idiom-function-adaptation | Translation idiom function adaptation | Source idiom in context;Intended communicative effect;Audience familiarity | Determine what the idiom accomplishes in the passage~Separate literal imagery from the intended meaning~Check whether a known target expression carries the same tone and implication~Prefer a plain paraphrase when a reliable equivalent is unavailable~Explain significant adaptation choices in the handoff | literal imagery would confuse the reader=>use a meaning-preserving paraphrase=>understanding is the relevant function~an alleged equivalent changes blame or intensity=>reject it=>similar imagery does not ensure similar force | Expression;Intended function;Adaptation;Caution | Fictional speaker Jo says We are on the same page after colleagues agree on the plan. | Same page;Agreement;We agree on the plan.;English gloss only~Tone;Collaborative;Keep inclusive we;Do not imply full agreement beyond plan | replacing an idiom with an unverified dictionary-like invention | Fictional writer uses a metaphor whose literal target-language reading would suggest a physical action.')
    add('GLA','translation-review-disagreement-resolution | Translation review disagreement resolution | Competing drafts;Source passage;Review criteria | Classify each disagreement as meaning, terminology, register or preference~Check meaning disputes against source context first~Compare terminology choices with approved references~Test stylistic alternatives against the stated audience and purpose~Record the selected wording and unresolved review responsibility | both versions preserve meaning and fit audience=>label preference separately=>personal taste is not an accuracy defect~source meaning remains contested=>escalate the source query=>voting cannot establish intended meaning | Dispute;Category;Evidence;Resolution | Fictional reviewers propose Please reply and Kindly respond for a peer message; both preserve the supplied request. | Reply versus respond;Style;Same requested action;Choose consistent house voice~Please versus kindly;Register preference;Peer audience;Ask target-language reviewer about local tone | counting every preference as a translation error | Fictional reviewers disagree about wording without stating whether meaning or style is at issue.')
    add('GLA','language-learner-error-feedback | Language learner error feedback | Learner sentence;Intended meaning;Learning focus | Ask what the learner intended if meaning is uncertain~Separate meaning-blocking problems from optional style changes~Correct only the selected learning focus first~Explain the pattern using the supplied sentence and a contrasting example~Ask for a new sentence to check transfer rather than copying | intended meaning is unclear=>ask before rewriting=>a grammatical sentence may express the wrong idea~many errors overwhelm the learner=>prioritize one recurring pattern=>feedback needs an actionable next attempt | Learner wording;Intended meaning;Feedback;Transfer task | Fictional learner Uma writes Yesterday I walk to school and confirms a completed past event. | Walk;Completed past action;Use walked in this sentence.;Write another completed event.~Yesterday;Past time cue;Keep the cue while changing the verb.;Explain how the wording matches time. | rewriting everything without showing the learner what changed | Fictional learner requests feedback on one recurring tense pattern in a short sentence.')
    add('GLA','language-practice-dialogue-scaffold | Language practice dialogue scaffold | Communicative situation;Learner ability;Target expressions | Define the practical outcome of the conversation~Choose expressions that directly support that outcome~Write a short model exchange with clear speaker roles~Remove selected support for a second attempt~Add an unexpected but plausible response that tests repair language | the learner memorizes without responding=>change one conversational condition=>flexible use matters beyond recitation~a phrase is beyond demonstrated ability=>offer a simpler equivalent=>practice should permit an independent response | Dialogue stage;Support;Learner action;Check | Fictional learner Bea practices asking a colleague to repeat an instruction in English. | Model;Could you repeat the last step?;Ask for clarification;Specific missing part named~Variation;Colleague speaks too quickly;Could you say that more slowly?;Repair request matches obstacle | calling memorized repetition a successful conversation | Fictional learner can repeat a model but cannot ask for clarification when the response changes.')
    domain('GPO','Poetry Craft and Revision','Creative Writing','Compose and revise poems through concrete imagery, lineation, sound and structural decisions.', 'Use original invented text for demonstrations. Describe craft through observable effects: image placement, repeated sounds, line endings, stanza breaks, syntax and changes in perspective. A craft device is a choice, not a guarantee of emotional impact. Preserve the user intended voice; do not impose rhyme, meter or a named form unless requested. Read drafts aloud or describe the intended pauses without pretending a recording was heard.')
    add('GPO','poem-image-bank-development | Poem image bank development | Subject or scene;Sensory observations;Emotional direction | Separate observed scene details from abstract emotion labels~Generate sensory images grounded in the chosen setting~Select images whose relationships suggest the intended feeling~Remove decorative images that introduce an unrelated scene~Arrange a small image bank with possible movement between details | an image depends on unavailable personal experience=>label it invented=>the poem should not imply a factual memory~all images repeat one sense=>test another relevant sensory detail=>variation can reveal a different aspect of the scene | Image;Sensory channel;Possible function;Connection | Fictional poet Mira invents an empty kitchen after a visitor leaves, with a cooling cup and a chair pushed back. | Cooling cup;Touch imagined;Suggests elapsed time;Visitor absence~Pushed-back chair;Sight;Traces recent presence;Connect to cup | naming loneliness instead of finding details that can carry it | Fictional poet asks for images of departure using an invented station scene.')
    add('GPO','poem-line-break-experiment | Poem line break experiment | Draft passage;Desired emphasis;Reading pace | Identify syntactic units in the passage~Mark words that could gain emphasis at a line ending~Create alternative lineations without changing the words~Compare suspense, double readings and breath across versions~Select breaks that support the intended movement and explain the tradeoff | a break creates an unintended reading=>move or exploit it deliberately=>line endings influence provisional meaning~every line ends at punctuation=>test a run-on break=>syntax and lineation need not always coincide | Break choice;Immediate reading;Effect;Decision | Fictional poet Ren drafts I leave the light on for the moth and wants leave to feel briefly like departure. | After leave;I leave;Momentary departure reading;Test deliberately~After on;Light remains on;More direct sequence;Use if ambiguity distracts | assuming short lines automatically create intensity | Fictional poet wants to compare two lineations of the same sentence without changing its wording.')
    add('GPO','poem-sound-pattern-revision | Poem sound pattern revision | Poem draft;Desired pace or texture;Words that must remain | Identify repeated consonant or vowel sounds in the supplied wording~Read the pattern in context rather than assigning universal emotional meanings~Generate a restrained alternative with a changed sound pattern~Compare clarity and emphasis before choosing the musical version~Keep meaningful words when a sound-driven substitution distorts the scene | sound repetition obscures meaning=>reduce the pattern=>music should not make the scene unintelligible~a rhyme forces a false detail=>replace the rhyme=>the chosen scene outranks convenient sound | Phrase;Sound feature;Alternative;Tradeoff | Fictional poet Nia writes Small stones slide and wants the motion to remain easy to hear. | Small stones slide;Repeated initial s sound;Loose pebbles shift;Alternative changes texture~Stones;Concrete object;Keep if scene requires stones;Do not choose only for sound | forcing every word to share an initial sound | Fictional poet asks for smoother sound while preserving the literal action of a draft.')
    add('GPO','poem-metaphor-coherence-review | Poem metaphor coherence review | Draft metaphor;Underlying subject;Surrounding images | Identify the subject and the image used to understand it~Map the properties actually transferred between them~Check later images for compatible or intentionally shifting logic~Remove explanations that merely repeat the metaphor~Revise mixed imagery when it distracts from the intended connection | a later verb belongs to a conflicting image system=>choose a coherent action or mark a deliberate shift=>unexamined mixtures can blur the relation~the metaphor implies an unwanted judgment=>change the transferred property=>imagery carries more than decoration | Image relation;Transferred property;Conflict;Revision | Fictional poet Ada calls a waiting room a harbor, then writes that it grows branches; no deliberate image shift is intended. | Waiting room as harbor;Temporary shelter;Branches introduce tree logic;Test boats or moorings~Waiting;Suspended movement;Fits harbor image;Keep the shared relation | adding more figurative language without checking what it implies | Fictional poet mixes a staircase and a weather system in one comparison and wants clearer imagery.')
    add('GPO','poem-stanza-turn-design | Poem stanza turn design | Draft sequence;Desired change;Existing stanza breaks | Identify the current emotional or argumentative direction~Choose the moment where perception, time or address changes~Place a stanza boundary where it makes that change legible~Test whether the turn is earned by earlier details~Revise the following stanza so it develops rather than restates the shift | a break has no change in function=>test joining the stanzas=>white space should serve the poem chosen movement~the turn introduces an unsupported explanation=>prepare it with a concrete detail=>a sudden claim may feel imposed | Stage;Current material;Turn function;Revision | Fictional poet Ivo describes a locked garden gate and then notices a seedling outside it. | Before turn;Locked gate;Focus on exclusion;End stanza at gate~After turn;Seedling outside;Shift toward persistence;Begin with the seedling | inserting a stanza break after every sentence without a reason | Fictional poet wants a change in perspective to emerge from an existing concrete detail.')
    add('GPO','poem-speaker-address-control | Poem speaker and address control | Draft pronouns;Speaker identity;Intended addressee | Identify who speaks and what the reader can infer about them~Track each you, we and they to a plausible referent~Mark shifts between self-address, another person and a wider audience~Choose whether ambiguity is intentional or needs a clarifying detail~Revise pronouns and address cues without inventing biography | you changes referent unintentionally=>add a cue or change the pronoun=>the reader needs a navigable address~speaker identity is deliberately open=>preserve relevant ambiguity=>clarity does not require a full biography | Address cue;Possible referent;Risk;Revision | Fictional poet Lea addresses an absent sister, then writes you never know to mean people generally. | Opening you;Sister;Clear from supplied context;Retain~You never know;General reader;Unmarked shift;Try no one knows | treating every first-person poem as the author autobiography | Fictional poet wants a dramatic speaker distinct from the author and a consistent addressee.')
    add('GPO','poem-repetition-variation-plan | Poem repetition and variation plan | Repeated phrase;Poem progression;Desired accumulation | Locate each recurrence and its surrounding context~Identify what changes in meaning between repetitions~Vary one element where the poem needs development~Remove repetitions that add neither pressure nor contrast~Check the final recurrence against the opening effect | repeated wording has identical function each time=>change context or remove one occurrence=>repetition needs a purpose in this draft~variation erases the recognizable pattern=>restore a stable anchor=>the reader must perceive recurrence | Recurrence;Context;Change;Purpose | Fictional poet Sora repeats The door stays open first for arrival and later after departure. | Opening;Someone expected;Welcome;Establish phrase~Return;Visitor gone;Absence changes meaning;Keep phrase with changed context | repeating a line solely to make the poem longer | Fictional poet wants a refrain to change meaning without changing every word.')
    add('GPO','poem-concrete-language-compression | Poem concrete language compression | Poem draft;Essential images;Meaning that must remain | Identify abstract explanation after a concrete image~Test removing the explanation while preserving the image~Replace weak general verbs with accurate actions from the scene~Cut redundant modifiers without adding a new fact~Compare the compressed draft with the intended meaning | a cut removes necessary context=>restore a concise cue=>compression is not obscurity~a stronger verb changes the action=>reject it=>precision matters more than intensity | Draft element;Function;Compression;Meaning check | Fictional poet Edda writes The empty chair was very empty and made me feel the absence of her presence. | Empty repeated;Redundant emphasis;Her chair stands empty.;Keeps absence~Emotional explanation;Names implied feeling;Test removing it;Reader sees chair detail | replacing every word with a dramatic synonym | Fictional poet wants a shorter draft while keeping the setting and implied relationship.')
    add('GPO','poem-ending-alternatives | Poem ending alternatives | Draft poem;Current ending;Desired openness | Identify what the poem has already established~Classify the current ending as image, statement, question or return~Draft alternatives that change the degree of closure~Check whether each alternative adds an unsupported moral~Choose the ending whose final emphasis fits the poem movement | the ending explains an image already clear=>test ending on the image=>repetition of meaning may weaken the final emphasis~an open ending removes essential context=>restore enough orientation=>ambiguity should remain interpretable | Ending option;Closure;Effect;Choice condition | Fictional poet Mina ends a scene with an unopened letter on a table and the line This means I am afraid. | Keep explanation;Explicit;Names emotion;Use only if direct declaration fits voice~End on letter;Open;Leaves action unresolved;Use if scene supports uncertainty | assuming every poem needs a universal lesson | Fictional poet asks for contrasting endings without changing the events already in the draft.')
    add('GPO','poem-reading-performance-markup | Poem reading performance markup | Final draft;Audience setting;Desired pacing | Mark syntax-based pauses separately from line breaks~Identify words needing emphasis because they change the meaning~Plan breath points that preserve complete sense units~Test a restrained reading against an exaggerated alternative~Produce a performance copy with a small consistent notation key | a line break interrupts meaning when spoken=>continue through it=>visual and spoken boundaries can differ~every word is marked for emphasis=>remove most marks=>contrast requires unmarked language | Mark;Meaning;Placement;Reason | Fictional performer Noor reads The door stays open for you and wants the invitation emphasized without pausing after open. | Underline;Emphasis;you;Names recipient~Slash;Pause;After complete sentence;Preserves invitation | inserting a dramatic pause at every line ending | Fictional performer needs a marked reading copy that does not change the poem words.')
    domain('GCO','Workplace Message Craft','Communication & Negotiation','Draft workplace messages that make requests, decisions, constraints and responsibilities explicit.', 'Use the relationship, authority and prior commitments supplied by the user. Drafting does not authorize sending. Keep assertions distinguishable from proposals, and never invent agreement, deadlines or availability. A useful message names its purpose, the relevant context and the response needed; its length and tone should fit the recipient. Avoid treating a script as a guarantee of agreement or conflict resolution.')
    add('GCO','workplace-request-specificity-rewrite | Workplace request specificity rewrite | Draft request;Needed outcome;Recipient role | Identify the action the sender actually needs~Separate essential context from background that obscures the ask~State the requested deliverable in observable terms~Distinguish an agreed deadline from a proposed timing request~Draft a message that lets the recipient accept, clarify or suggest an alternative | timing was never agreed=>label it a request=>a sender preference is not a commitment~recipient authority is unclear=>ask who can decide=>a clear message cannot create authority | Message element;Current issue;Draft wording;Response needed | Fictional sender Mira writes Please handle the notes to a colleague and needs unresolved decisions identified; no deadline is agreed. | Action;Handle is vague;Could you identify the unresolved decisions in these notes?;Confirm scope~Timing;Not agreed;Please let me know when you could return that list.;Propose feasible timing | presenting a new deadline as previously agreed | Fictional sender wants a clearer request without assuming the recipient has already accepted the work.')
    add('GCO','workplace-decline-with-alternative | Workplace decline with alternative | Incoming request;Actual constraint;Available alternatives | Identify the part of the request that cannot be accepted~State the constraint without inventing personal details~Separate a firm boundary from an optional alternative~Offer only help the sender can actually provide~Close with the choice or next contact needed from the requester | no alternative is available=>decline clearly without manufacturing one=>false offers create new obligations~the request is partly feasible=>name the accepted portion precisely=>partial acceptance should not imply full ownership | Request part;Constraint;Response;Next choice | Fictional colleague Asha cannot lead a workshop but can review its outline; no availability date is supplied. | Facilitation;Cannot accept;I cannot lead the workshop.;Find another facilitator~Outline;Review possible;I can review the outline if that would help.;Agree scope and timing | saying yes politely while intending not to do the task | Fictional employee needs to decline an extra role while offering a smaller contribution they can genuinely make.')
    add('GCO','workplace-scope-renegotiation-message | Workplace scope renegotiation message | Existing commitment;New constraint;Feasible options | Restate the actual agreed deliverable~Describe the new constraint and its practical effect~Develop scope or timing options supported by supplied capacity~Explain the tradeoff for each option~Ask the decision owner to choose before silently changing the commitment | capacity estimates are uncertain=>offer a checkpoint rather than a guaranteed date=>another unsupported promise compounds the problem~the change affects dependent work=>identify that dependency=>renegotiation needs consequences visible | Commitment;Constraint;Option;Decision needed | Fictional designer Ren promised a full guide but can now deliver an outline while an essential source remains unavailable. | Full guide;Missing source;Provide outline first;Does staged delivery meet the need?~Final guide;Source dependent;Reassess after source arrives;Agree next checkpoint | quietly reducing scope and calling the commitment complete | Fictional worker must renegotiate a deliverable after learning that a required input will not be available.')
    add('GCO','workplace-disagreement-message | Workplace disagreement message | Proposed decision;Specific concern;Shared objective | Restate the proposal in terms its author would recognize~Identify the precise point of disagreement~Support the concern with supplied observations rather than motives~Offer a test, modification or alternative tied to the shared objective~Invite correction of facts and a decision on the disputed point | disagreement depends on an assumption=>label the assumption=>a concern is not an established defect~the objection targets personality=>rewrite it around behavior or impact=>personal attribution does not resolve the decision | Proposal;Concern;Evidence boundary;Draft response | Fictional team plans online-only signups; colleague Nia knows some intended participants lack reliable internet but has no count. | Online-only signup;Access barrier;Some participants reported difficulty;Could we retain a non-online route?~Scale of barrier;Count unknown;Do not estimate prevalence;Can we check access needs before deciding? | attributing bad motives to the proposer | Fictional colleague disagrees with a process proposal and wants a specific evidence-based response.')
    add('GCO','workplace-feedback-observation-request | Workplace feedback observation request | Observed behavior;Work impact;Desired future action | Describe the specific behavior without a personality label~Separate observed impact from inferred intent~State the change needed in the next comparable situation~Ask for the other person perspective on relevant constraints~Draft feedback with a concrete follow-up observation | the evidence is secondhand=>attribute it and seek confirmation=>hearsay should not become a direct accusation~the requested change is vague=>name observable behavior=>the recipient needs to know what to do differently | Observation;Impact;Request;Follow-up | Fictional colleague Bea received a handoff note without an owner field and had to ask who should act next. | Missing owner;Additional clarification needed;Please include the next owner in handoff notes.;Check next handoff~Intent;Not known;Ask whether the template makes ownership unclear.;Discuss template issue | calling someone careless instead of naming the missing information | Fictional manager wants feedback on a documented handoff omission without guessing the employee motive.')
    add('GCO','workplace-status-update-draft | Workplace status update draft | Completed work;Current blockers;Recipient decision needs | Separate completed outcomes from work merely started~Identify the blocker that changes timing or scope~State what the sender is doing within current authority~Name the decision or input needed from the recipient~Draft a concise update with a realistic next checkpoint | completion evidence is missing=>report work in progress=>activity does not prove delivery~no recipient action is needed=>say so explicitly=>a status message should not imply a hidden request | Status element;Evidence;Draft wording;Needed response | Fictional analyst Jo has organized source notes but cannot complete a summary until a missing report arrives. | Completed;Notes organized;The source notes are organized.;None~Blocked;Report absent;The summary is waiting on the report. Can you confirm who can provide it?;Identify source owner | reporting a percentage complete without a defensible basis | Fictional contributor needs to report progress and one dependency without inventing a delivery date.')
    add('GCO','workplace-decision-record-message | Workplace decision record message | Meeting notes;Explicit decisions;Open questions | Extract decisions actually made rather than proposals merely discussed~Attach owners and conditions only where agreed~Separate unresolved points from the decision list~Draft a confirmation message inviting factual corrections~Identify where the accepted record should be retained | notes do not establish agreement=>label the item proposed=>silence in rough notes is not approval~an owner was not assigned=>mark unassigned=>recording a name can create a false obligation | Item;Meeting status;Message wording;Open point | Fictional team agreed to test a shorter form but did not choose an owner or launch date. | Shorter-form test;Agreed;We agreed to test a shorter form.;Scope details pending~Owner and timing;Not agreed;The owner and timing remain unassigned.;Who will coordinate the next step? | turning every suggestion in notes into a decision | Fictional note-taker needs a confirmation message distinguishing one agreement from unresolved implementation details.')
    add('GCO','workplace-introduction-request | Workplace introduction request | People and relationship;Reason for connection;Permission status | Explain why the proposed connection is relevant to both parties~Separate known interests from assumed willingness~Ask permission before sharing private contact details~Draft a concise introduction using only authorized information~Leave the recipients free to decline without implied endorsement | permission to introduce is missing=>draft a permission request first=>knowing someone does not authorize sharing their details~the sender cannot vouch for a claim=>omit or attribute it=>an introduction should not manufacture endorsement | Introduction element;Known fact;Draft;Permission check | Fictional organizer Mira knows Ren works on volunteer scheduling and Bea wants to learn about that work; neither has agreed to an introduction. | Relevance;Shared scheduling topic;Would you be open to an introduction about volunteer scheduling?;Ask each person~Contact details;Not authorized;Do not include private details yet.;Wait for permission | sharing a contact list as part of a casual introduction | Fictional colleague wants to connect two people but has not asked either whether they welcome the connection.')
    add('GCO','workplace-clarification-without-blame | Workplace clarification without blame | Ambiguous instruction;Work blocked;Available interpretations | Quote or paraphrase the ambiguous part accurately~State the plausible interpretations without ridiculing either~Explain which next action depends on clarification~Ask the smallest question that resolves the branch~Offer a provisional action only if it is safe and clearly reversible | a guess would cause consequential rework=>wait for clarification=>speed does not justify avoidable commitment~one independent task can proceed=>identify it separately=>a blocked decision need not stop unrelated preparation | Ambiguity;Interpretations;Question;Independent work | Fictional colleague Lio is told update the final copy but finds a draft file and an approved file with similar names. | Final copy;Draft or approved version;Which file should I update?;List proposed edits separately~Authority;Approval may matter;Should the approved version remain locked?;Do not overwrite either | implying the sender was careless instead of asking a resolvable question | Fictional worker needs to clarify which document can be edited before making changes.')
    add('GCO','workplace-recipient-action-edit | Workplace recipient action edit | Long message;Recipient responsibilities;Essential context | Identify every action implied by the message~Group actions by recipient rather than by drafting order~Remove background that does not affect the requested response~Make dependencies and optional information visually distinct~Check the revised message for one clear response path | several people receive different asks=>name each role beside its action=>a group greeting can hide responsibility~background includes an unresolved dependency=>retain it near the relevant ask=>shortening must not erase a blocker | Message part;Recipient need;Edit;Response path | Fictional sender Ada writes a long project history but only needs a reviewer to confirm whether an outline covers the requested topics. | History;Not needed for this check;Move to optional background.;Review outline~Main ask;Topic coverage;Does this outline cover the requested topics? Please identify any missing topic.;Return missing topics or confirm coverage | cutting all context until the ask becomes impossible to judge | Fictional sender wants a shorter message that preserves the evidence needed for the recipient decision.')

